      United States time zones are standardized geographic regions regulated by the federal government to establish uniform civil time. The standard time system aligns community schedules with regional daylight patterns, preventing local clock variances across states.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today, the U.S. Department of Transportation (DOT) has federal jurisdiction over timezone boundaries. The DOT reviews boundary change requests from state and local authorities, evaluating how proposed changes affect the "convenience of commerce." This evaluation checks retail habits, transit schedules, television signals, and regional trade patterns to ensure boundary lines serve economic safety and efficiency.
    </p>

