  <section id="history-of-us-timezones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="History of US Time Zones">
    <h2>2. History of Time Zones in the United States</h2>
    <p>
      The history of American timekeeping transitioned from localized solar tracking to modern federal synchronization:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Railroad Standard Time (1883)</strong>: Prior to 1883, the U.S. had over 300 local time zones. Railroad operators found scheduling timetables nearly impossible. On November 18, 1883, major rail corporations unilaterally established four standard time zones (Eastern, Central, Mountain, and Pacific), coordinating clocks at exactly noon on what became known as the "Day of Two Noons."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Standard Time Act of 1918</strong>: Congress officially formalized the timezone boundaries and established federal authority under the Interstate Commerce Commission. This act also introduced the nation's first Daylight Saving Time rules to conserve fuel during World War I.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Uniform Time Act of 1966</strong>: Due to local variations (where states and cities changed clocks on different dates), Congress enacted this law to establish uniform start and end dates for DST across the nation, while allowing states to opt out via state legislation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Energy Policy Act of 2005</strong>: This act extended the duration of U.S. Daylight Saving Time, shifting the start to the second Sunday of March and the end to the first Sunday of November, which added four weeks of DST compared to historical schedules.
      </li>
    </ul>

