  <section id="overview-of-us-timezones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="U.S. Time Zones Overview">
    <h2>3. Complete Overview of U.S. Time Zones</h2>
    <p>
      The six primary time zones observed in the 50 states are detailed below:
    </p>

    <!-- Eastern Time -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Eastern Time (ET)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>UTC Offsets:</strong> UTC-5 (EST) during standard time (winter); UTC-4 (EDT) during daylight saving time (summer).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Coverage:</strong> Eastern Time is observed in 17 East Coast states in their entirety (Connecticut, Delaware, Georgia, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, New Jersey, New York, North Carolina, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, South Carolina, Vermont, Virginia, and West Virginia) and parts of 6 others (Florida, Indiana, Kentucky, Michigan, Tennessee, and Alabama's unofficial Phenix City). Major metropolitan hubs include New York City, Boston, Washington D.C., Miami, Atlanta, Detroit, and Philadelphia.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business and Media Impact:</strong> Eastern Time serves as the primary benchmark for U.S. business, media broadcasting, and financial markets. Major national television networks (ABC, CBS, NBC, FOX) schedule their primetime releases based on Eastern Time hours. The New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) and NASDAQ open at 9:30 AM Eastern Time and close at 4:00 PM Eastern Time, forcing West Coast traders and international brokers to align their daily work schedules with Wall Street clocks.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Examples &amp; Conversions:</strong> During the winter, when New York is on EST (UTC-5), London (on GMT, UTC+0) is 5 hours ahead. If it is 12:00 PM EST in New York, it is 5:00 PM GMT in London. In the summer, when New York transitions to EDT (UTC-4) and London transitions to BST (UTC+1), the 5-hour offset remains. If it is 12:00 PM EDT in New York, it is 5:00 PM BST in London.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Central Time -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Central Time (CT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>UTC Offsets:</strong> UTC-6 (CST) during standard time (winter); UTC-5 (CDT) during daylight saving time (summer).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Coverage:</strong> Central Time is observed in 10 states in their entirety (Alabama, Arkansas, Illinois, Iowa, Louisiana, Minnesota, Mississippi, Missouri, Oklahoma, and Wisconsin) and parts of 10 others (Florida, Indiana, Kansas, Kentucky, Michigan, Nebraska, North Dakota, South Dakota, Tennessee, and Texas). Major cities include Chicago, Houston, Dallas, San Antonio, New Orleans, Memphis, Nashville, and Minneapolis.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business and Media Impact:</strong> Central Time represents the industrial, agricultural, and logistics heartland of North America. Chicago serves as the global hub for commodities futures trading via the Chicago Mercantile Exchange (CME). In American broadcasting, prime-time television is standardly announced as "9:00 PM Eastern, 8:00 PM Central" (9/8c), meaning Central Time viewers watch prime-time programming one hour earlier in local solar time to synchronize nationwide broadcasts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Examples &amp; Conversions:</strong> When Chicago is on CST (UTC-6) in the winter, it is 6 hours behind London (GMT). If it is 10:00 AM CST in Chicago, it is 4:00 PM GMT in London. During summer, when Chicago is on CDT (UTC-5) and New York is on EDT (UTC-4), Central Time remains exactly 1 hour behind Eastern Time.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Mountain Time -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Mountain Time (MT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>UTC Offsets:</strong> UTC-7 (MST) during standard time (winter); UTC-6 (MDT) during daylight saving time (summer).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Coverage:</strong> Mountain Time is observed in 6 states in their entirety (Colorado, Montana, New Mexico, Utah, Wyoming, and Arizona—though Arizona does not use DST) and parts of 8 others (Idaho, Kansas, Nebraska, Nevada, North Dakota, Oregon, South Dakota, and Texas). Major cities include Denver, Salt Lake City, Phoenix, Boise, El Paso, and Albuquerque.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business and Geographic Impact:</strong> Mountain Time covers the vast Rocky Mountain corridor. Denver, known as the "Mile High City," serves as the major financial, telecommunications, and aerospace hub of this zone. Because Arizona remains on MST (UTC-7) year-round, it shares the same clock hours as Denver in the winter, but matches Los Angeles (PDT, UTC-7) during the summer, requiring careful calendar adjustments for regional businesses.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Examples &amp; Conversions:</strong> When Denver is on MDT (UTC-6) in July, it is 6 hours behind UTC. If it is 1:00 PM MDT in Denver, it is 7:00 PM UTC. At the same time, Phoenix (on MST, UTC-7) is at 12:00 PM, one hour behind Denver.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Pacific Time -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Pacific Time (PT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>UTC Offsets:</strong> UTC-8 (PST) during standard time (winter); UTC-7 (PDT) during daylight saving time (summer).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Coverage:</strong> Pacific Time is observed in 4 states in their entirety (California, Washington, Nevada—except West Wendover, and Oregon—except part of Malheur County) and the northern panhandle of Idaho. Major metropolitan areas include Los Angeles, San Francisco, Seattle, San Diego, Las Vegas, Portland, and Sacramento.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business and Technology Impact:</strong> Pacific Time is the global epicenter for the technology, entertainment, and biotechnology sectors. Silicon Valley (housing Apple, Google, and Meta) and Seattle (housing Microsoft and Amazon) drive global software and cloud standards. Pacific Time corporations coordinate extensively with Asian markets (like Tokyo, Seoul, and Sydney) across the Pacific Ocean corridor.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Examples &amp; Conversions:</strong> When Los Angeles is on PDT (UTC-7) in August, it is 3 hours behind New York (EDT, UTC-4). If a video call is scheduled for 10:00 AM PDT in Los Angeles, New York participants will join at 1:00 PM EDT.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Alaska Time -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Alaska Time (AKT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>UTC Offsets:</strong> UTC-9 (AKST) during standard time (winter); UTC-8 (AKDT) during daylight saving time (summer).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Coverage:</strong> Alaska Time is observed across the entire state of Alaska, except for the westernmost Aleutian Islands (which observe Hawaii-Aleutian Time). Major cities include Anchorage, Fairbanks, and Juneau (the state capital).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Consolidation History:</strong> Prior to 1983, the massive state of Alaska spanned four different time zones (Pacific, Yukon, Alaska-Hawaii, and Bering Time). To simplify commerce, communications, and state administration, the state government consolidated these into just two zones in 1983, placing 99% of the population under a single Alaska Time standard.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Examples &amp; Conversions:</strong> When Anchorage is on AKST (UTC-9) in December, it is 4 hours behind New York (EST, UTC-5). If it is 8:00 AM AKST in Anchorage, it is 12:00 PM EST in New York.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Hawaii-Aleutian Time -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Hawaii–Aleutian Time (HAT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>UTC Offsets:</strong> UTC-10 (HST) year-round in Hawaii (no DST); UTC-10 (HAST) / UTC-9 (HADT) in the western Aleutian Islands of Alaska.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Coverage:</strong> Observed in the island state of Hawaii and the westernmost islands of Alaska's Aleutian chain (such as Adak and Shemya). Major cities include Honolulu, Hilo, Kailua, and Adak.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Daylight Saving Exemptions:</strong> Hawaii does not observe Daylight Saving Time. Because of its tropical location near the equator, Hawaii experiences nearly equal day and night lengths year-round, making seasonal clock shifts unnecessary. This creates a seasonal offset shift relative to the U.S. mainland: Hawaii is 5 hours behind Eastern Time in the winter (EST) but becomes 6 hours behind in the summer (EDT).
      </p>
    </article>

