       SECTION 8: DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME IN THE UNITED STATES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="dst-in-usa" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Daylight Saving Time USA">
    <h2>6. Daylight Saving Time in the United States</h2>
    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time (DST) is the seasonal practice of advancing clocks by one hour in the spring and reversing them in the autumn. Under federal law, DST begins on the second Sunday of March and ends on the first Sunday of November:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Spring Forward</strong>: At 2:00 AM on the second Sunday of March, clocks advance immediately to 3:00 AM, skipping one hour and creating a 23-hour day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fall Back</strong>: At 2:00 AM on the first Sunday of November, clocks fall back immediately to 1:00 AM, repeating that hour and creating a 25-hour day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Arizona and Hawaii Opt-Outs</strong>: Hawaii opted out of DST because of its low latitude, where daylight length varies minimally. Arizona opted out in 1968 to avoid extending late-afternoon heat during desert summers. However, the Navajo Nation in northeastern Arizona does observe DST, while the Hopi Reservation (which lies entirely within the Navajo Nation) does not.
      </li>
    </ul>

