  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 11: PROGRAMMING & TECHNOLOGY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="programming-technology" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Programming &amp; Technology">
    <h2>9. Programming &amp; Technology</h2>
    <p>
      Managing time zone calculations in software is critical to prevent data synchronization issues. Standard software architectures include:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>UTC Database Storing</strong>: All databases (e.g. PostgreSQL, MySQL) must store timestamps in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) using ISO 8601 formatting: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`. Local offsets are applied only on the client front-end.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>IANA Named Time Zones</strong>: In code, developers refer to location names (e.g., `America/New_York` or `America/Los_Angeles`) rather than hardcoded offsets, ensuring that transition shifts update automatically based on local laws.
      </li>
    </ul>

