  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 12: COMMON MISTAKES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Time Zone Mistakes">
    <h2>10. Common Mistakes</h2>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 1: Using EST in the Summer</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Writing "EST" (Eastern Standard Time) in July is incorrect. The Eastern Time Zone observes EDT (Eastern Daylight Time, UTC-4) in summer. Meeting schedulers using automated software will book calls one hour late for international participants. Use "ET" to refer to the zone generally.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 2: Assuming Arizona is Always on Mountain Time</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Because Arizona does not observe DST, it operates on MST (UTC-7) year-round. In the summer, MST shares the same offset as Pacific Daylight Time (PDT, UTC-7).
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Mistake 3: Scheduling During Transition Weeks</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> The United States changes clocks on the second Sunday of March, while the UK and Europe change on the last Sunday of March. This temporary gap shifts the standard 5-hour New York-London offset to 4 hours for two weeks.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

